    "product_bottom_faq_hhq647": {
      "type": "product-bottom-faq",
      "blocks": {
        "left_6WrLgq": {
          "type": "left",
          "settings": {
            "accordion_heading1": "I’m using a hormonal contraceptive",
            "dsc1": "<p>While you can certainly take Monthlies powder for its potential benefits, it's important to note that hormonal contraceptives, such as implants and pills, can affect hormone levels.  </p><p>Since hormonal contraceptives regulate your hormone balance, the efficacy of the powder might be compromised by the contraceptive method you are using.</p>"
          }
        },
        "left_pePbY6": {
          "type": "left",
          "settings": {
            "accordion_heading1": "I’m on a fertility treatment",
            "dsc1": "<p>Fertility treatments often involve precise hormone manipulation.</p><p>It's vital to recognise that introducing new supplements, including the Monthlies powder, should be done under the guidance of your healthcare provider. The introduction of external supplements could potentially disrupt the intended hormonal adjustments of the treatment.</p>"
          }
        },
        "left_Whw3MP": {
          "type": "left",
          "settings": {
            "accordion_heading1": "I’m already taking supplements",
            "dsc1": "<p>It depends on what other supplements you are taking and why. Good complements to monthlies could be Omega-3s, Probiotics, and Evening Primrose<br/>oil, depending on your specific needs. Please consult your doctor or reach out to us in chat for more information.</p>"
          }
        },
        "right_jJrCAD": {
          "type": "right",
          "settings": {
            "accordion_heading": "I have PCOS",
            "dsc": "<p>Yes, you can take monthlies if you have PCOS, as there are many ingredients that may benefit those with PCOS. </p><p>However, monthlies is not meant to treat any disease, so you should always check with your doctor before incorporating new supplements into your routine. Also, check for possible interactions with your medications (if any).</p>"
          }
        },
        "right_mKU3mh": {
          "type": "right",
          "settings": {
            "accordion_heading": "I have endometriosis",
            "dsc": "<p>Yes, you can take monthlies if you have endometriosis. However, monthlies is a natural supplement and not designed to treat any illness. Always ask your doctor before adding any new supplements to your routine, and check for possible interactions with medications.</p>"
          }
        },
        "right_MTMG8w": {
          "type": "right",
          "settings": {
            "accordion_heading": "I am breastfeeding or pregnant",
            "dsc": "<p>It is not recommended to take monthlies if you are<br/>breastfeeding or pregnant, </p>"
          }
        },
        "left_hba7R6": {
          "type": "left",
          "settings": {
            "accordion_heading1": "I am trying to get pregnant",
            "dsc1": "<p>Yes, you can take monthlies if you are trying to get pregnant. Although monthlies is not a prenatal supplement, it does have the recommended dose of folate for those of reproductive age (400 mcg). It’s important to remember to stop monthlies once you find out you are pregnant</p>"
          }
        }
      },
